# Emergency Stop Runbook

> Use this when an AI agent has made destructive or unwanted changes.

## Scenario 1: Uncommitted Changes (agent wrote bad code)

```bash
# Discard all unstaged changes
git restore .

# Remove untracked files the agent created
git clean -fd

# Verify clean state
git status
```

## Scenario 2: Committed but NOT Pushed

```bash
# Undo last commit, keep changes staged (safe — lets you review)
git reset --soft HEAD~1

# Undo last commit AND discard changes (destructive)
git reset --hard HEAD~1

# Undo last N commits
git reset --soft HEAD~3
```

## Scenario 3: Already Pushed

```bash
# Create a revert commit (safe — preserves history)
git revert <commit-sha>
git push

# Revert a range of commits
git revert <oldest-sha>..<newest-sha>
git push
```

## Scenario 4: Unknown Corruption (git history damaged)

```bash
# View full history including resets
git reflog

# Find last known good state
git log --oneline --all | head -20

# Restore to a known good state
git reset --hard <good-commit-sha>
```

## Scenario 5: Agent Deleted Important Files

```bash
# Restore specific file from last commit
git checkout HEAD -- path/to/file

# Restore file from specific commit
git checkout <commit-sha> -- path/to/file

# Find when a file was deleted
git log --diff-filter=D --summary | grep "path/to/file"
```

## Prevention Checklist

- [ ] PreToolUse hook blocks `rm -rf`, `git reset --hard`, `git push --force`
- [ ] Agent runs in OS sandbox (sandbox-exec / bwrap)
- [ ] CODEOWNERS requires human review for Red tier paths
- [ ] CI runs Gitleaks before merge
